#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb77f8d2-d61f-5fbf-8b0d-cdb85ee1150f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|A|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293972} {:answer/id #uuid "d0aebf3c-1c48-50be-88ad-0db1ff223fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293973} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4c41d7-4a36-56f3-9ee4-d0d09f1dceeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293974} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac00cae-5b52-54d3-9553-d12fba3f37a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293975} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4b6b13-375a-53ef-a3b2-6a5771e3b446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec4c41d7-4a36-56f3-9ee4-d0d09f1dceeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293974} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a96d4ac-7bf1-5b43-81c4-f075da7140cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3923b7ab-8feb-5de3-adf5-945f64f8af6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-156191" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{-e}^{0}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =({e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-e}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-e}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-e}^{0}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ A+{\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
